40 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n"}, {"content": "Since `calibration_curve` is already defined in `sklearn/calibration.py` and does not directly mention a plotting function, we need to find where the plotting might occur or where we can add the new plotting function.\n\nLet's search for files that contain plotting functionalities, specifically looking for terms like `matplotlib` or `plt`.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"matplotlib\", \"plt\"]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py:check_matplotlib_support\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py:PartialDependenceDisplay.plot\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.__init__\n##Searching for term \"matplotlib\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from itertools import product\n  2 | \n  3 | import numpy as np\n  4 | \n  5 | from .. import confusion_matrix\n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | from ...utils import deprecated\n  8 | from ...utils.multiclass import unique_labels\n  9 | from ...base import is_classifier\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | class ConfusionMatrixDisplay:\n 13 |     \"\"\"Confusion Matrix visualization.\n 14 | \n 15 |     It is recommend to use\n 16 |     :func:`~sklearn.metrics.ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_estimator` or\n 17 |     :func:`~sklearn.metrics.ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions` to\n 18 |     create a :class:`ConfusionMatrixDisplay`. All parameters are stored as\n 19 |     attributes.\n 20 | \n 21 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 22 | \n 23 |     Parameters\n 24 |     ----------\n 25 |     confusion_matrix : ndarray of shape (n_classes, n_classes)\n 26 |         Confusion matrix.\n 27 | \n 28 |     display_labels : ndarray of shape (n_classes,), default=None\n 29 |         Display labels for plot. If None, display labels are set from 0 to\n 30 |         `n_classes - 1`.\n 31 | \n 32 |     Attributes\n 33 |     ----------\n 34 |     im_ : matplotlib AxesImage\n 35 |         Image representing the confusion matrix.\n 36 | \n 37 |     text_ : ndarray of shape (n_classes, n_classes), dtype=matplotlib Text, \\\n 38 |             or None\n 39 |         Array of matplotlib axes. `None` if `include_values` is false.\n 40 | \n 41 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 42 |         Axes with confusion matrix.\n 43 | \n 44 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 45 |         Figure containing the confusion matrix.\n 46 | \n 47 |     See Also\n 48 |     --------\n 49 |     confusion_matrix : Compute Confusion Matrix to evaluate the accuracy of a\n 50 |         classification.\n 51 |     ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_estimator : Plot the confusion matrix\n 52 |         given an estimator, the data, and the label.\n 53 |     ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions : Plot the confusion matrix\n 54 |         given the true and predicted labels.\n 55 | \n 56 |     Examples\n 57 |     --------\n 58 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 60 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y,\n 65 |     ...                                                     random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0)\n 67 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 68 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n 69 |     >>> predictions = clf.predict(X_test)\n 70 |     >>> cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, predictions, labels=clf.classes_)\n 71 |     >>> disp = ConfusionMatrixDisplay(confusion_matrix=cm,\n 72 |     ...                               display_labels=clf.classes_)\n 73 |     >>> disp.plot()\n 74 |     <...>\n 75 |     >>> plt.show()\n 76 |     \"\"\"\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from .base import _get_response\n  2 | \n  3 | from .. import average_precision_score\n  4 | from .. import precision_recall_curve\n  5 | \n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | \n  8 | \n  9 | class PrecisionRecallDisplay:\n 10 |     \"\"\"Precision Recall visualization.\n 11 | \n 12 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.plot_precision_recall_curve`\n 13 |     to create a visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 14 | \n 15 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 16 | \n 17 |     Parameters\n 18 |     -----------\n 19 |     precision : ndarray\n 20 |         Precision values.\n 21 | \n 22 |     recall : ndarray\n 23 |         Recall values.\n 24 | \n 25 |     average_precision : float, default=None\n 26 |         Average precision. If None, the average precision is not shown.\n 27 | \n 28 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 29 |         Name of estimator. If None, then the estimator name is not shown.\n 30 | \n 31 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 32 |         The class considered as the positive class. If None, the class will not\n 33 |         be shown in the legend.\n 34 | \n 35 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 36 | \n 37 |     Attributes\n 38 |     ----------\n 39 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 40 |         Precision recall curve.\n 41 | \n 42 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 43 |         Axes with precision recall curve.\n 44 | \n 45 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 46 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 47 | \n 48 |     See Also\n 49 |     --------\n 50 |     precision_recall_curve : Compute precision-recall pairs for different\n 51 |         probability thresholds.\n 52 |     plot_precision_recall_curve : Plot Precision Recall Curve for binary\n 53 |         classifiers.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Examples\n 56 |     --------\n 57 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import (precision_recall_curve,\n 60 |     ...                              PrecisionRecallDisplay)\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y,\n 65 |     ...                                                     random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0)\n 67 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 68 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n 69 |     >>> predictions = clf.predict(X_test)\n 70